diff --git a/docs/deployment.md b/docs/deployment.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..47c627b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy
+
+Fizzy ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) configuration for running a
+complete instance on a single Linux server. Everything runs in one container:
+
+- Rails (Puma and Thruster) serves the app.
+- Solid Queue background workers run inside Puma.
+- SQLite (primary, cable, cache, and queue databases) and Active Storage uploads
+  live on a persistent Docker volume, so they survive deployments.
+
+The Kamal proxy obtains a Let's Encrypt TLS certificate for your hostname
+automatically, so visitors get `https://your-host` with no separate web server to
+configure.
+
+## What you'll need
+
+- A Linux server (x86_64 or ARM) with SSH access and Docker. Kamal installs Docker
+  during `bin/kamal setup` if it isn't already installed.
+- Ruby and Bundler on the machine you deploy from, so you can run `bundle install`
+  and `bin/kamal`.
+- A hostname (for example `fizzy.example.com`) with its DNS `A` record pointing
+  at the server.
+- Ports 80 and 443 open to the internet, so Let's Encrypt can validate the
+  hostname and the proxy can serve traffic.
+- An SMTP server for Fizzy's magic-link sign-in emails, such as Postmark,
+  Fastmail, or your own mail server.
+- An account on a container registry (Docker Hub by default) where the image
+  will be pushed.
+
+## 1. Configure the deployment
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml` and replace the placeholders:
+
+- `image` and `registry.username`: the registry namespace where you push, e.g.
+  your Docker Hub username.
+- `servers.web`: your server's IP address or hostname.
+- `proxy.host`: the hostname Fizzy will be served at. This is also the value for
+  `APP_FQDN` under `env.clear`, which is used for links in emails and
+  notifications.
+- `env.clear`: your mail server's `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, and
+  `SMTP_USERNAME`, plus the `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` emails should come from.
+- `builder.arch`: change `amd64` to `arm64` if your server is ARM.
+
+Kamal supports many other mailer options via `env.clear` if your provider needs
+them (`SMTP_AUTHENTICATION`, `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO`, etc.).
+
+## 2. Configure your secrets
+
+If you haven't already, install the local gems:
+
+```sh
+bundle install
+```
+
+Fizzy needs a stable secret for signing cookies, sessions, and links. The
+shipped `.kamal/secrets` generates one on the first deploy and caches it in
+`tmp/secret_key_base` (which is gitignored), so existing sessions stay valid
+across later deploys.
+
+If you prefer to manage app secrets with Rails credentials instead, create them
+once with:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails credentials:edit
+```
+
+That writes `config/master.key` (gitignored) and
+`config/credentials.yml.enc`; the master key is passed to the container
+automatically.
+
+The remaining secrets are your registry and mail passwords. Provide them either
+directly in your shell before deploying:
+
+```sh
+export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=your-registry-token
+export SMTP_PASSWORD=your-smtp-password
+```
+
+or by replacing those lines in `.kamal/secrets` with commands that read them
+from your password manager.
+
+## 3. Deploy
+
+Install [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) if you don't already have it, then run
+the first deployment from the repository root:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This bootstraps Docker on the server, builds and pushes the image, prepares the
+databases, and starts Fizzy. When it finishes, Fizzy is available at
+`https://your-host`.
+
+Afterwards, deploy new versions with:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+## Data, backups, and jobs
+
+The `fizzy:/rails/storage` volume declared in `config/deploy.yml` holds the
+SQLite databases and uploaded files. Kamal reattaches the same volume on every
+deploy, so data survives upgrades and restarts. To move to a new server or take
+a backup, back up that Docker volume.
+
+Background jobs (notifications, cleanup, recurring tasks) run in the same
+container as the web server, because `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA` is enabled and Puma
+starts Solid Queue for you. There is no separate worker container to manage.
+
+## Useful commands
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs               # tail application logs
+bin/kamal app exec -i "bin/rails console"
+bin/kamal app exec -i "bin/rails dbconsole"
+bin/kamal rollback           # roll back to the previous version
+```
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- **Certificate not issued**: confirm the DNS record points at the server and
+  that ports 80/443 are reachable. Check `bin/kamal proxy logs`.
+- **Sign-in emails not arriving**: check the `SMTP_*` values in
+  `config/deploy.yml` and the `SMTP_PASSWORD` secret. `bin/kamal logs` will show
+  delivery errors.
+- **Starting over**: `bin/kamal remove` stops and removes the app, and
+  `docker volume rm fizzy` deletes the data volume. This is irreversible.
